<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mobutu Sese Seko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1930–1997</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mobutu Sese Seko was the ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, which he renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a>, from 1965 to 1997. An army officer, he seized power in a coup in November 1965 and ruled as an authoritarian president backed by Western powers during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, promoting a policy of 'authenticity' while his regime became a byword for corruption. He was overthrown in 1997 by Laurent-Désiré Kabila's rebels, backed by Rwanda and Uganda, and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko.jpg" alt="Mobutu Sese Seko" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Hall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mobutu_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
